2026-07-14T08:19:05.6265437Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6265708Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6266159Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6266531Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6266733Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6266972Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6267169Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6267401Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6267610Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6267868Z         def inc(x):
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6268095Z             return x + 1
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6268313Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6268633Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6268839Z         def test_answer():
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6269101Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6269337Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6269517Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6269711Z     To execute it::
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6269928Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6270114Z         $ pytest
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6270394Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6270740Z         collected 1 items
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6270963Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6271163Z         test_sample.py F
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6271385Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6271637Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6272033Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6272359Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6272561Z             def test_answer():
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6272807Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6273061Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6273379Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6273640Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6273855Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6274193Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6274517Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6274702Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6275473Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6276301Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6276484Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6276670Z     Features
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6276878Z     --------
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6277175Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6277700Z     - Detailed info on failing `assert statements <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/assert.html>`_ (no need to remember ``self.assert*`` names)
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6278283Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6278584Z     - `Auto-discovery
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6279001Z       <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/goodpractices.html#python-test-discovery>`_
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6279451Z       of test modules and functions
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6279699Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6280049Z     - `Modular fixtures <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/fixtures.html>`_ for
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6280531Z       managing small or parametrized long-lived test resources
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6280848Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6281192Z     - Can run `unittest <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/unittest.html>`_ (or trial)
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6281606Z       test suites out of the box
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6281849Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6282047Z     - Python 3.10+ or PyPy3
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6282291Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6282807Z     - Rich plugin architecture, with over 1300+ `external plugins <https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/plugin_list.html>`_ and thriving community
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6283436Z     
2026-07-14T08:19:05.6283626Z     
